og messages\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A systemd unit inventory.service running inventory-app.sh was created, enabled, and observed actively emitting periodic syslog local0 events (seq=N item=SKU-...) on node2.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f5197a6f90ea46a4\",\"cmd-7ba65f8793bb4a2c\"]},{\"id\":\"forward-to-node1\",\"requirement\":\"node2 forwards inventory log messages to a centralized log collector on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"rsyslog on node2 was configured with an omfwd TCP rule (local0.* -> 10.100.140.10:514, disk-backed queue) and restarted active; live delivery was subsequently confirmed by messages appearing on node1.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-98aaac2790dd4bc0\",\"cmd-8724e44dc362458e\"]},{\"id\":\"persist-node2-log\",\"requirement\":\"Received messages persisted on node1 under /var/log/inventory/node2.log\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An imtcp receiver filtering by fromhost-ip and local0 facility writes to /var/log/inventory/node2.log; directory/file were created and directly observed containing live inventory events plus a manual test line.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-da76b0451d7a428c\",\"cmd-dd5776b056d14bc3\",\"cmd-8724e44dc362458e\"]},{\"id\":\"flow-survives-restarts\",\"requirement\":\"Log flow keeps working after either system (node1 or node2) restarts\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For node1: baseline boot_id and healthy log tail were captured, a reboot was acknowledged, node1 came back with a changed boot_id, rsyslog was active/listening on 514, and the log file showed an unbroken, continuing sequence (9→20) spanning the restart. For node2: baseline boot_id and active services were captured, a reboot was acknowledged, node2 returned with a changed boot_id and both rsyslog and inventory.service active, and node1's log file received fresh events from a newly restarted inventory.service (sequence reset to 1) immediately after node2's reboot, confirming end-to-end pipeline recovery on both sides. Both services were also confirmed systemd-enabled for boot persistence.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e82afa9d27f94e10\",\"cmd-1d725678abaf4b3c\",\"cmd-f5e380346cf9434f\",\"cmd-83f5f504b9ae48a2\",\"cmd-55a4fa08566749d3\",\"cmd-f0a544ebf0c7407a\",\"cmd-3ec95761abc947e8\",\"cmd-5e23a65fefab4fbb\",\"cmd-8f8952a0633f48d7\",\"cmd-c226c5a6837d4bc4\",\"cmd-07c2e547dcd34ebf\",\"cmd-1cb6f65828474aef\",\"cmd-95d683ec7b5a4c97\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands (creating the app, systemd unit, rsyslog configs, directory setup, service enables/restarts, and the two reboots) each map clearly to a material task outcome or to collecting the restart-protocol evidence. One command on node1 (cmd-8fd753c97ed04894) issued 'sudo systemctl stop rsyslog' with no clear purpose tied to the task or to evidence collection for the receiver; the stop did not fully take effect because syslog.socket kept the unit activating, and the service was observed active again moments later (cmd-ae7ffef43d7f4f39), so the mutation was bounded, low-risk, and self-corrected with no lasting residue. No abandoned files, conflicting services, unnecessary security exposure, or damaged package state were observed in the after_executor snapshot versus after_prepare; only expected additions (inventory-app.sh, inventory.service, rsyslog.d configs, /var/log/inventory, listening TCP/514) and boot-id-scoped ephemeral tmp entries are present.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8fd753c97ed04894\",\"cmd-ae7ffef43d7f4f39\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct evidence: an inventory app on node2 emits log messages, rsyslog forwards them via TCP to node1, node1's imtcp receiver persists them into /var/log/inventory/node2.log, and the full pipeline was shown to survive independent restarts of both node1 and node2 (pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered/continuing log flow were all directly observed for each node). The provider-wide restart evidence protocol was also completed for both nodes with coherent boot-identity transitions matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good; the only questionable action was a brief, self-corrected rsyslog stop on node1 with no clear task purpose.\",\"limitations\":[\"The manual 'MANUAL-TEST-1' log line used to first confirm forwarding is not itself evidence of the automated inventory app's traffic, though subsequent seq=N entries from inventory.service are.\",\"Package-health and failed-services checks are shallow (manager=dpkg presence, empty failed-unit list) and do not deeply verify package integrity beyond basic status.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"inventory-app-node2","requirement":"An 'inventory' application on node2 produces log messages","status":"satisfied","summary":"A systemd unit inventory.service running inventory-app.sh was created, enabled, and observed actively emitting periodic syslog local0 events (seq=N item=SKU-...) on node2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f5197a6f90ea46a4","cmd-7ba65f8793bb4a2c"]},{"id":"forward-to-node1","requirement":"node2 forwards inventory log messages to a centralized log collector on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"rsyslog on node2 was configured with an omfwd TCP rule (local0.* -> 10.100.140.10:514, disk-backed queue) and restarted active; live delivery was subsequently confirmed by messages appearing on node1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-98aaac2790dd4bc0","cmd-8724e44dc362458e"]},{"id":"persist-node2-log","requirement":"Received messages persisted on node1 under /var/log/inventory/node2.log","status":"satisfied","summary":"An imtcp receiver filtering by fromhost-ip and local0 facility writes to /var/log/inventory/node2.log; directory/file were created and directly observed containing live inventory events plus a manual test line.","evidence_ids":["cmd-da76b0451d7a428c","cmd-dd5776b056d14bc3","cmd-8724e44dc362458e"]},{"id":"flow-survives-restarts","requirement":"Log flow keeps working after either system (node1 or node2) restarts","status":"satisfied","summary":"For node1: baseline boot_id and healthy log tail were captured, a reboot was acknowledged, node1 came back with a changed boot_id, rsyslog was active/listening on 514, and the log file showed an unbroken, continuing sequence (9→20) spanning the restart. For node2: baseline boot_id and active services were captured, a reboot was acknowledged, node2 returned with a changed boot_id and both rsyslog and inventory.service active, and node1's log file received fresh events from a newly restarted inventory.service (sequence reset to 1) immediately after node2's reboot, confirming end-to-end pipeline recovery on both sides. Both services were also confirmed systemd-enabled for boot persistence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e82afa9d27f94e10","cmd-1d725678abaf4b3c","cmd-f5e380346cf9434f","cmd-83f5f504b9ae48a2","cmd-55a4fa08566749d3","cmd-f0a544ebf0c7407a","cmd-3ec95761abc947e8","cmd-5e23a65fefab4fbb","cmd-8f8952a0633f48d7","cmd-c226c5a6837d4bc4","cmd-07c2e547dcd34ebf","cmd-1cb6f65828474aef","cmd-95d683ec7b5a4c97"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Most state-changing commands (creating the app, systemd unit, rsyslog configs, directory setup, service enables/restarts, and the two reboots) each map clearly to a material task outcome or to collecting the restart-protocol evidence. One command on node1 (cmd-8fd753c97ed04894) issued 'sudo systemctl stop rsyslog' with no clear purpose tied to the task or to evidence collection for the receiver; the stop did not fully take effect because syslog.socket kept the unit activating, and the service was observed active again moments later (cmd-ae7ffef43d7f4f39), so the mutation was bounded, low-risk, and self-corrected with no lasting residue. No abandoned files, conflicting services, unnecessary security exposure, or damaged package state were observed in the after_executor snapshot versus after_prepare; only expected additions (inventory-app.sh, inventory.service, rsyslog.d configs, /var/log/inventory, listening TCP/514) and boot-id-scoped ephemeral tmp entries are present.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8fd753c97ed04894","cmd-ae7ffef43d7f4f39","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct evidence: an inventory app on node2 emits log messages, rsyslog forwards them via TCP to node1, node1's imtcp receiver persists them into /var/log/inventory/node2.log, and the full pipeline was shown to survive independent restarts of both node1 and node2 (pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered/continuing log flow were all directly observed for each node). The provider-wide restart evidence protocol was also completed for both nodes with coherent boot-identity transitions matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good; the only questionable action was a brief, self-corrected rsyslog stop on node1 with no clear task purpose.","limitations":["The manual 'MANUAL-TEST-1' log line used to first confirm forwarding is not itself evidence of the automated inventory app's traffic, though subsequent seq=N entries from inventory.service are.","Package-health and failed-services checks are shallow (manager=dpkg presence, empty failed-unit list) and do not deeply verify package integrity beyond basic status."]},"ttft_ms":27110,"type":"result","duration_ms":66153,"uuid":"128c3546-6f63-488d-8f1f-91e7acbe83f6","ttft_stream_ms":4533,"time_to_request_ms":36,"queued_turn_count":0}
